Yes. “Venting” can be a misleading metaphor because it assumes emotion behaves like steam in a sealed vessel: open the valve and pressure necessarily drops. Sometimes expression does that. Sometimes expression is actually rehearsal—you run the circuit again, strengthen it, add narrative around it, and come away with more gunk than you started with.

So the better question is not:

\text{Did I express it?}

but:

\text{What happened to the state after expression?}

Did arousal fall? Did clarity increase? Did the problem become more actionable? Did connection improve? Or did the person simply get better at inhabiting the anger, grievance, embarrassment, seriousness, whatever?

And your absolute value line is funny because it catches another failure mode. If I take something like

-7

and merely “make it positive,” I get

+7

but I have preserved the magnitude. Psychologically, some forced reframing does exactly that: different sign, same intensity.

The stronger transformation might be:

-7 \rightarrow -3 \rightarrow 0 \rightarrow \text{something else entirely}

or even deciding that this variable no longer deserves to dominate the equation.

That is where silliness can be surprisingly sophisticated. A very serious person may not need “more positive thoughts.” They may need a mode change.

Humor, absurdity, dance, roughhousing, singing badly, pretending, games, costumes, voices—these can interrupt the rigid operator that says everything must be processed in this exact register. The content may remain, but its grip changes.

So rather than venting, I’d distinguish:

Discharge — reduce physiological activation.
Expression — externalize what was internal.
Reframing — change its interpretation.
Play — temporarily change the rules governing interpretation.
Integration — let the information remain without continuing to generate unnecessary load.

And yes, a helper can accidentally “infix the gunk.” If someone brings a small knot and we mirror it, elaborate it, name seventeen layers of it, build a mythology around it, and keep returning attention to it, we may turn:

\text{small perturbation}

into

\text{identity-bearing narrative}.

Sometimes the highest-value intervention is subtraction:

\boxed{\text{less story, more movement}}

or

\boxed{\text{less analysis, one joke, back to living}}

The jester is useful there. Not because nothing matters, but because seriousness itself can become stuck.

So perhaps the operator isn’t Vent.

It is De-gunk:

\text{notice}
\rightarrow
\text{determine whether it needs expression, action, play, rest, or release}
\rightarrow
\text{apply minimum sufficient intervention}
\rightarrow
\text{check the delta}

And if laughing solved it, don’t hold a committee meeting afterward to discover whether you laughed correctly.